1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,990
Have you heard? Canada wants
to join the European Union.

2
00:00:04,829 --> 00:00:08,839
What a nonsense. How is Canada
supposed to move to Europe?

3
00:00:09,727 --> 00:00:14,027
Except that in 2026, the president
of the European Commission stood

4
00:00:14,107 --> 00:00:18,577
up in the European Parliament and
proposed more or less exactly that.

5
00:00:19,295 --> 00:00:21,285
Not as a joke. As policy.

6
00:00:22,207 --> 00:00:26,177
Not full membership. Something
that does not exist yet.

7
00:00:26,687 --> 00:00:29,517
Canada as the Union's
first associate member.

8
00:00:32,157 --> 00:00:34,407
And here is the part most people miss.

9
00:00:35,297 --> 00:00:36,967
Canada is already halfway in.

10
00:00:37,673 --> 00:00:41,643
In February 2026, Canada
joined something called SAFE.

11
00:00:42,527 --> 00:00:45,357
That is the Union's joint
scheme for arming itself.

12
00:00:46,407 --> 00:00:50,897
A hundred and fifty billion euros of
borrowed money, spent on weapons together.

13
00:00:51,713 --> 00:00:55,303
Canada is the first country
outside Europe ever allowed in.

14
00:00:55,990 --> 00:00:57,800
That did not come out of nowhere.

15
00:00:58,730 --> 00:01:03,160
The summer before, Canada and the Union
signed a security and defence partnership.

16
00:01:04,045 --> 00:01:09,315
In 2024, Canada joined Horizon Europe,
the Union's research programme.

17
00:01:10,325 --> 00:01:13,155
Canadian labs now apply
for European money.

18
00:01:13,935 --> 00:01:18,805
And since 2017, goods have crossed the
Atlantic under a joint trade agreement.

19
00:01:19,663 --> 00:01:24,453
So the question was never whether Canada
can work with Europe. It already does.

20
00:01:27,253 --> 00:01:29,383
The question is how far this goes.

21
00:01:30,272 --> 00:01:33,742
And that turns out to be a question
about three words in a treaty.

22
00:01:34,571 --> 00:01:38,901
The European Union has a rule
about who may join. It is short.

23
00:01:39,789 --> 00:01:40,829
Any European State...

24
00:01:40,909 --> 00:01:43,719
may apply to become a member of the Union.

25
00:01:44,599 --> 00:01:46,029
Any European State.

26
00:01:47,021 --> 00:01:48,851
Canada is a great many things.

27
00:01:49,501 --> 00:01:51,611
A European state is not one of them.

28
00:01:53,991 --> 00:01:55,641
Although notice something.

29
00:01:56,540 --> 00:01:58,590
That sentence is about membership.

30
00:01:59,601 --> 00:02:03,371
The research programme Canada joined
never said anything of the kind.

31
00:02:04,281 --> 00:02:08,171
Israel has been in it for
years. So has New Zealand.

32
00:02:08,917 --> 00:02:10,867
Geography guards the front door.

33
00:02:11,477 --> 00:02:13,387
It never guarded the side entrances.

34
00:02:16,477 --> 00:02:18,467
So what is actually being proposed?

35
00:02:19,560 --> 00:02:20,790
Picture two doors.

36
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:25,340
Behind the first one: full
membership. A seat at the table.

37
00:02:25,420 --> 00:02:26,900
A vote. A veto.

38
00:02:26,980 --> 00:02:29,510
And every obligation that comes with it.

39
00:02:30,180 --> 00:02:33,230
Under the treaties as they
stand, that door is shut.

40
00:02:34,073 --> 00:02:38,733
Behind the second one: an ordinary
partner. Trade deals. Joint projects.

41
00:02:38,813 --> 00:02:40,623
Everything Canada already has.

42
00:02:41,425 --> 00:02:43,135
That door is wide open.

43
00:02:43,745 --> 00:02:45,775
It just doesn't take you anywhere new.

44
00:02:46,579 --> 00:02:49,449
So Europe is talking about
building a third door.

45
00:02:50,259 --> 00:02:52,089
One that isn't in the building yet.

46
00:02:52,927 --> 00:02:56,137
The list of what might go through
it is already circulating.

47
00:02:57,757 --> 00:02:59,247
Defence. Energy. Critical minerals.

48
00:02:59,437 --> 00:03:03,057
Artificial intelligence. Quantum
technology. Cybersecurity.

49
00:03:03,137 --> 00:03:04,567
And the Arctic.

50
00:03:07,367 --> 00:03:11,257
But the moment you draw a new door,
you have to say how wide it opens.

51
00:03:12,135 --> 00:03:14,905
How much of the single
market would Canada get?

52
00:03:15,751 --> 00:03:18,501
Which European rules would
Canada have to follow?

53
00:03:19,260 --> 00:03:23,590
Would Canadian firms sell into Europe
on the same terms as German ones?

54
00:03:24,391 --> 00:03:25,601
Would Canada pay in?

55
00:03:26,705 --> 00:03:28,795
And then the one that decides everything.

56
00:03:29,703 --> 00:03:32,553
Would Canada get a say in
the rules it has to obey?

57
00:03:33,447 --> 00:03:35,237
And this is not hypothetical.

58
00:03:36,273 --> 00:03:38,473
Europe has started charging imported steel

59
00:03:38,553 --> 00:03:41,623
and cement for the carbon
burnt to make them.

60
00:03:42,279 --> 00:03:45,519
A Canadian smelter that
wants to sell into Europe now

61
00:03:45,599 --> 00:03:48,589
fills in its paperwork under
a rule written in Brussels.

62
00:03:49,564 --> 00:03:51,314
It pays. It does not vote.

63
00:03:54,254 --> 00:03:56,324
Norway is not in the European Union.

64
00:03:57,094 --> 00:04:00,144
Norwegians voted against joining. Twice.

65
00:04:01,138 --> 00:04:05,848
But since 1994, Norway has been
inside most of the single market.

66
00:04:06,610 --> 00:04:08,490
Norwegian goods move freely.

67
00:04:08,570 --> 00:04:11,240
Norwegian citizens move freely.

68
00:04:11,850 --> 00:04:13,500
Norway pays into the budget.

69
00:04:14,369 --> 00:04:16,559
And Norway takes on European rules.

70
00:04:16,689 --> 00:04:20,159
More than thirteen thousand
of them since 1994.

71
00:04:21,208 --> 00:04:23,838
Written in Brussels. Voted on in Brussels.

72
00:04:24,228 --> 00:04:26,898
By people Norwegians do not elect.

73
00:04:27,791 --> 00:04:29,251
Norwegians have a phrase for it.

74
00:04:29,331 --> 00:04:32,641
Fax democracy. The rules arrive. You sign.

75
00:04:35,277 --> 00:04:38,107
So Europe already comes
in more than two sizes.

76
00:04:39,021 --> 00:04:40,651
There is in. There is out.

77
00:04:40,901 --> 00:04:43,231
And there is a whole staircase in between.

78
00:04:44,068 --> 00:04:47,258
But every step on that staircase
has been standing in Europe.

79
00:04:48,068 --> 00:04:50,778
Canada would be the first
one standing somewhere else.

80
00:04:53,228 --> 00:04:54,848
By the way, if you'd like to see where the

81
00:04:54,928 --> 00:04:57,778
next question takes us,
consider subscribing.

82
00:04:58,688 --> 00:05:00,138
It really helps the channel.

83
00:05:00,921 --> 00:05:03,041
Which raises the obvious question.

84
00:05:03,121 --> 00:05:05,251
Why would Europe want that?

85
00:05:06,075 --> 00:05:08,585
Start with the map, and
then keep zooming out.

86
00:05:09,413 --> 00:05:11,723
Canada reaches far into the Arctic.

87
00:05:12,645 --> 00:05:14,345
It has the longest coastline of any

88
00:05:14,425 --> 00:05:17,495
country on Earth, and
most of it faces ice.

89
00:05:18,395 --> 00:05:21,655
As that ice thins, the ocean
underneath turns into shipping

90
00:05:21,735 --> 00:05:26,145
lanes, seabed and borders that
nobody has finished arguing about.

91
00:05:26,896 --> 00:05:29,106
Europe has Arctic coastline too.

92
00:05:29,636 --> 00:05:31,346
It would rather not argue alone.

93
00:05:34,056 --> 00:05:36,606
Then there is what sits
under the Canadian ground.

94
00:05:37,544 --> 00:05:39,324
Nickel. Cobalt. Copper.

95
00:05:39,404 --> 00:05:41,184
Rare earth elements.

96
00:05:41,264 --> 00:05:45,104
The unglamorous metals
inside every battery, every

97
00:05:45,184 --> 00:05:47,914
turbine in the North Sea,
every guided missile.

98
00:05:48,846 --> 00:05:51,876
Europe has the factories
and almost none of the ore.

99
00:05:52,611 --> 00:05:54,991
And Europe has spent four
years learning what it costs

100
00:05:55,071 --> 00:05:58,121
to buy something essential
from a single supplier.

101
00:06:00,901 --> 00:06:03,591
Now turn the map around
and look at it from Ottawa.

102
00:06:04,517 --> 00:06:08,627
Canada sells about three quarters of
everything it exports to one country.

103
00:06:09,435 --> 00:06:10,665
The United States.

104
00:06:11,745 --> 00:06:13,995
That is not a trading relationship.

105
00:06:14,745 --> 00:06:17,295
That is a dependency
with a very long border.

106
00:06:18,115 --> 00:06:21,155
And Canada has spent recent
years discovering that the

107
00:06:21,235 --> 00:06:24,605
terms of that relationship
can change without warning.

108
00:06:25,293 --> 00:06:29,183
And Europe has been having a version
of the same conversation with itself.

109
00:06:30,040 --> 00:06:31,120
Spending more on defence.

110
00:06:31,200 --> 00:06:36,670
Asking out loud how much of its own
security it ought to be able to provide.

111
00:06:37,583 --> 00:06:39,393
Neither of them says it loudly.

112
00:06:40,283 --> 00:06:41,913
Both of them are doing the arithmetic.

113
00:06:44,493 --> 00:06:47,863
Canada is not going to replace
the United States with Europe.

114
00:06:48,733 --> 00:06:50,083
That is not the proposal.

115
00:06:51,077 --> 00:06:54,887
It doesn't have to. It just has
to be less alone in the room.

116
00:06:57,787 --> 00:06:59,477
So both sides want this.

117
00:06:59,567 --> 00:07:02,637
Which is exactly when it
is worth slowing down.

118
00:07:03,453 --> 00:07:06,343
Because the awkward questions
have not gone anywhere.

119
00:07:07,111 --> 00:07:11,061
Imagine a European law that
lands on a Canadian company.

120
00:07:11,858 --> 00:07:13,348
Canada follows the rule.

121
00:07:13,838 --> 00:07:17,308
Canada did not vote on the
rule. Is that acceptable?

122
00:07:18,166 --> 00:07:19,366
Now flip it.

123
00:07:20,225 --> 00:07:24,135
Imagine Canada getting real
influence over European decisions.

124
00:07:25,142 --> 00:07:28,142
Would a voter in Portugal
accept that a country on

125
00:07:28,222 --> 00:07:31,672
another continent helped
write the law that binds them?

126
00:07:32,342 --> 00:07:36,702
And if the answer to both is a carefully
worded maybe, how many different kinds

127
00:07:36,782 --> 00:07:41,232
of membership can one union hold before
nobody can say what membership means?

128
00:07:42,081 --> 00:07:43,791
That is the whole argument.

129
00:07:44,779 --> 00:07:46,509
Who is in? And who decides?

130
00:07:49,678 --> 00:07:51,528
Which brings us back to the name.

131
00:07:52,398 --> 00:07:53,598
The European Union.

132
00:07:54,458 --> 00:07:57,248
Those two words only ever
run in one direction.

133
00:07:58,298 --> 00:08:01,448
European, therefore Europe,
therefore European countries.

134
00:08:02,385 --> 00:08:04,695
But look at what the second word is doing.

135
00:08:05,489 --> 00:08:06,805
Union.

136
00:08:07,255 --> 00:08:09,005
A union is not a place.

137
00:08:09,795 --> 00:08:12,425
It is an agreement about
what its members do together.

138
00:08:13,284 --> 00:08:16,134
Think about what the
European Union actually is.

139
00:08:16,921 --> 00:08:18,881
Common rules. A common market.

140
00:08:18,961 --> 00:08:20,611
Shared courts.

141
00:08:20,721 --> 00:08:22,941
Money moving without permission.

142
00:08:23,021 --> 00:08:26,911
Standards written once and
obeyed in twenty-seven countries.

143
00:08:27,754 --> 00:08:30,544
Not one of those things
is inherently European.

144
00:08:31,498 --> 00:08:35,248
A country on the other side of the
Atlantic can have compatible courts.

145
00:08:36,078 --> 00:08:37,788
It can meet the same standards.

146
00:08:38,558 --> 00:08:40,188
It can agree to the same rules.

147
00:08:43,118 --> 00:08:47,128
The project began in
1951 with six neighbours

148
00:08:47,438 --> 00:08:49,668
and an agreement about coal and steel.

149
00:08:50,425 --> 00:08:52,315
Neighbours was the starting point.

150
00:08:53,065 --> 00:08:55,435
The question is whether
it is also the ceiling.

151
00:08:56,219 --> 00:08:57,609
Nothing has been agreed.

152
00:08:58,179 --> 00:09:00,819
This is a proposal, and
proposals in Brussels

153
00:09:00,899 --> 00:09:03,549
have a long and difficult
life ahead of them.

154
00:09:04,231 --> 00:09:06,691
There is a summit in
October that is supposed

155
00:09:06,771 --> 00:09:09,181
to turn the idea into
something with edges.

156
00:09:10,109 --> 00:09:13,939
Canada may end up with a slightly
better trade deal and nothing more.

157
00:09:14,685 --> 00:09:16,975
But the interesting part already happened.

158
00:09:17,831 --> 00:09:22,221
Europe said out loud that membership might
not have to mean what it has always meant.

159
00:09:23,005 --> 00:09:25,235
Canada does not have to move to Europe.

160
00:09:25,505 --> 00:09:27,695
Europe does not have to move to Canada.

161
00:09:28,519 --> 00:09:32,969
For seventy-five years, European
integration answered one question.

162
00:09:33,879 --> 00:09:36,209
How do we get European
countries to cooperate?

163
00:09:37,215 --> 00:09:38,905
The next question is different.

164
00:09:39,755 --> 00:09:43,405
How do we get countries to cooperate
when they are not European at all?

165
00:09:44,139 --> 00:09:47,389
Maybe the E tells us where
the European Union came from.

166
00:09:48,054 --> 00:09:50,764
And maybe the U tells us
what it could still become.
